1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,170
Aarav reads how chips are made.

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:06,473
First, silicon is cleaned until
it is purer than almost anything.

3
00:00:06,822 --> 00:00:09,542
Then the factory adds impurity back in.

4
00:00:09,542 --> 00:00:10,497
On purpose!

5
00:00:10,997 --> 00:00:13,482
Why clean it, just to make it dirty again?

6
00:00:13,832 --> 00:00:17,973
Because the right impurity turns
silicon into a useful conductor.

7
00:00:18,623 --> 00:00:22,712
Think of a class where every student
holds hands with four friends.

8
00:00:23,062 --> 00:00:25,298
Nobody is free to walk around.

9
00:00:25,298 --> 00:00:27,524
Everyone is busy holding hands.

10
00:00:27,874 --> 00:00:29,228
That is pure silicon.

11
00:00:29,228 --> 00:00:31,302
Each atom has four outer electrons.

12
00:00:31,902 --> 00:00:34,036
It shares them with four neighbours.

13
00:00:34,036 --> 00:00:36,222
Each shared pair is a covalent bond.

14
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:40,211
Now warm the crystal a
little, to room temperature.

15
00:00:40,561 --> 00:00:42,185
Heat shakes one bond loose.

16
00:00:42,185 --> 00:00:44,466
An electron breaks free and wanders off.

17
00:00:45,066 --> 00:00:47,107
It leaves behind an empty spot.

18
00:00:47,107 --> 00:00:48,476
We call it a hole.

19
00:00:49,076 --> 00:00:51,383
A hole acts like a positive charge.

20
00:00:51,733 --> 00:00:53,806
A neighbour's electron hops into the hole.

21
00:00:54,156 --> 00:00:57,008
Now the hole has moved
one step the other way.

22
00:00:57,608 --> 00:01:01,379
Like an empty seat moving down
a cinema row as people shift.

23
00:01:01,979 --> 00:01:05,758
Every free electron made this
way leaves exactly one hole.

24
00:01:06,108 --> 00:01:09,873
So in pure silicon, electrons and
holes are always equal in number.

25
00:01:10,473 --> 00:01:13,301
We write n e equals n h equals n i.

26
00:01:13,901 --> 00:01:16,873
n i is the intrinsic
carrier concentration.

27
00:01:16,873 --> 00:01:19,781
Intrinsic means pure, with nothing added.

28
00:01:20,381 --> 00:01:23,900
For silicon at room
temperature, n i is tiny.

29
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:28,408
About one point five times ten
to the sixteen per cubic metre.

30
00:01:29,058 --> 00:01:30,252
That sounds big.

31
00:01:30,252 --> 00:01:33,407
But a cubic metre of
silicon has far more atoms.

32
00:01:34,007 --> 00:01:36,437
Five times ten to the
twenty eight of them.

33
00:01:37,087 --> 00:01:41,071
So only about one atom in three
trillion frees an electron.

34
00:01:41,671 --> 00:01:44,667
That is why pure silicon
is a poor conductor.

35
00:01:45,317 --> 00:01:46,835
Now we add impurity.

36
00:01:46,835 --> 00:01:48,454
This is called doping.

37
00:01:48,804 --> 00:01:52,754
Silicon with impurity added
is an extrinsic semiconductor.

38
00:01:53,104 --> 00:01:55,314
First, add a few arsenic atoms.

39
00:01:55,664 --> 00:01:56,865
Arsenic is pentavalent.

40
00:01:56,865 --> 00:01:58,855
That means it has five outer electrons.

41
00:01:59,455 --> 00:02:01,959
Four of them join bonds with silicon.

42
00:02:01,959 --> 00:02:04,006
The fifth one has no partner.

43
00:02:04,606 --> 00:02:09,430
A tiny push, about point zero
five electron volts, sets it free.

44
00:02:10,030 --> 00:02:13,004
Arsenic donates an electron,
so it is called a donor.

45
00:02:13,604 --> 00:02:15,227
Now electrons far outnumber holes.

46
00:02:15,227 --> 00:02:16,127
This is n-type.

47
00:02:16,042 --> 00:02:17,738
Electrons are the majority carriers.

48
00:02:18,388 --> 00:02:20,120
Next, add indium instead.

49
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:21,641
Indium is trivalent.

50
00:02:21,641 --> 00:02:23,533
It has only three outer electrons.

51
00:02:24,133 --> 00:02:26,793
One bond is left with a missing electron.

52
00:02:26,793 --> 00:02:28,516
That is a ready-made hole.

53
00:02:29,116 --> 00:02:33,372
Indium accepts an electron to fill
it, so it is called an acceptor.

54
00:02:33,972 --> 00:02:35,598
Now holes are the majority.

55
00:02:35,598 --> 00:02:36,565
This is p-type.

56
00:02:36,565 --> 00:02:38,268
Electrons become the minority.

57
00:02:38,918 --> 00:02:41,139
A pinch of impurity brings
a flood of carriers.

58
00:02:41,939 --> 00:02:43,575
Let's see it on the breadboard.

59
00:02:43,925 --> 00:02:47,380
A battery drives current through
a small block of silicon.

60
00:02:47,730 --> 00:02:48,936
Pure silicon first.

61
00:02:48,936 --> 00:02:50,962
The current on the scope is tiny.

62
00:02:51,312 --> 00:02:52,374
Now dope it.

63
00:02:52,374 --> 00:02:55,921
One arsenic atom for every
ten million silicon atoms.

64
00:02:56,271 --> 00:02:57,523
Watch the scope jump.

65
00:02:57,523 --> 00:02:59,524
Free electrons pour into the circuit.

66
00:03:00,174 --> 00:03:01,395
Look at the readout.

67
00:03:01,395 --> 00:03:02,821
Electrons went up hugely.

68
00:03:03,171 --> 00:03:04,578
But holes went down.

69
00:03:04,578 --> 00:03:06,031
Why would holes drop?

70
00:03:06,681 --> 00:03:07,972
Think of a seesaw.

71
00:03:08,322 --> 00:03:11,738
More free electrons means more of
them fall into holes and fill them.

72
00:03:12,338 --> 00:03:14,681
This filling is called recombination.

73
00:03:14,681 --> 00:03:16,340
So the holes get fewer.

74
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:20,537
The rule is n e times
n h equals n i squared.

75
00:03:21,137 --> 00:03:23,296
It is called the mass action law.

76
00:03:23,296 --> 00:03:25,931
It holds in pure and doped crystals alike.

77
00:03:26,581 --> 00:03:27,649
Worked example.

78
00:03:27,649 --> 00:03:29,262
Our doped silicon block.

79
00:03:29,612 --> 00:03:34,128
Silicon has five times ten to the
twenty eight atoms per cubic metre.

80
00:03:34,728 --> 00:03:39,528
One arsenic per ten million gives five
times ten to the twenty one donors.

81
00:03:40,228 --> 00:03:42,324
Each donor gives one electron.

82
00:03:42,324 --> 00:03:45,452
So n e is five times
ten to the twenty one.

83
00:03:46,152 --> 00:03:49,861
Holes: n h equals n i
squared divided by n e.

84
00:03:50,561 --> 00:03:53,685
Square one point five
times ten to the sixteen.

85
00:03:54,385 --> 00:03:58,033
That gives two point two five
times ten to the thirty two.

86
00:03:58,733 --> 00:04:01,562
Divide by five times
ten to the twenty one.

87
00:04:02,262 --> 00:04:05,452
n h is four point five
times ten to the ten.

88
00:04:06,152 --> 00:04:09,247
Electrons now beat holes by
about a hundred billion to one.

89
00:04:09,897 --> 00:04:11,358
Now, the exam trap.

90
00:04:11,708 --> 00:04:15,227
Many students say n-type
silicon is negatively charged.

91
00:04:15,227 --> 00:04:16,181
It is not.

92
00:04:16,531 --> 00:04:20,824
Each extra electron leaves behind
a fixed positive arsenic ion.

93
00:04:20,824 --> 00:04:22,745
The crystal stays neutral.

94
00:04:23,395 --> 00:04:24,456
Where is this asked?

95
00:04:24,456 --> 00:04:27,091
PUC, CBSE and ISC boards ask
about doping and carriers.

96
00:04:27,441 --> 00:04:31,890
JEE Main uses the mass action
law, as in July twenty twenty one.

97
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:33,440
Try this.

98
00:04:33,353 --> 00:04:37,446
Germanium has n i of two point
four times ten to the nineteen.

99
00:04:37,446 --> 00:04:42,104
It is doped with indium, four point
eight times ten to the twenty one per

100
00:04:42,104 --> 00:04:43,065
cubic metre.

101
00:04:43,065 --> 00:04:44,334
Which is correct?

102
00:04:49,734 --> 00:04:50,634
A.

103
00:04:50,083 --> 00:04:54,164
It is p-type, with electrons at one
point two times ten to the seventeen.

104
00:04:54,514 --> 00:04:57,009
Indium is trivalent, an acceptor.

105
00:04:57,009 --> 00:05:00,307
So holes are the majority,
and it is p-type.

106
00:05:00,657 --> 00:05:05,253
n h is about equal to the acceptor
count, four point eight times ten to

107
00:05:05,253 --> 00:05:06,432
the twenty one.

108
00:05:06,782 --> 00:05:09,044
n e is n i squared over n h.

109
00:05:09,044 --> 00:05:12,607
That gives one point two
times ten to the seventeen.

110
00:05:13,257 --> 00:05:14,803
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:18,003 --> 00:05:19,668
Next, the p-n junction.

112
00:05:19,668 --> 00:05:22,544
What happens where p-type
silicon meets n-type?
